2026-05-02

## Issue

I ran into a compiler issue (bug?) when replacing crt0.o provided by bcc with
custom C runtime code. When compiling a program with an argc/argv `main`
signature, a dangling `.globl ___mkargv` declaration is left in the code:

```
int main(argc, argv)
char **argv;
{
    int i;

    printf("arg count: %d\n", argc);
    for (i=0; i < argc; i++) {
        printf("arg %d: %s\n", i, argv[i]);
    }

    return 42;
}
```

The generated assembly file declares a global reference to `___mkargv_`, as
shown below, but it isn't called anywhere.
```
! 129 # 3 "main.c"
! 3 int main(argc, argv)
! 4 char **argv;
.text
export	_main
_main:
!BCC_EOS
! 5 {
.globl	___mkargv
! 6     int i;
!BCC_EOS
! 7 
! 8     printf("arg count: %d\n", argc);
```

Leading to the following linker error:
```
../../vendor/bcc/bin/ld86 -0 -s -L../../lib -Cso -lc main.o -Oend.o -o argv
undefined symbol: ___mkargv
make: *** [Makefile:26: argv] Error 1
```

## Solution

Since the MINIX `crtso.o` code already handles setting up arc/argv for main, we
don't need the `___mkargv` routine in our code. I hacked around this by
creating a small assembly file with a trivial definition for `___mkargc`
routine (even though it's never called), which was enough to please the linker:

```
; hack.s
.globl	___mkargv
___mkargv: jmp ___mkargv    ; we should never get here
```

Success!
```
../../vendor/bcc/bin/ld86 -0 -s -L../../lib -Cso -lc main.o hack.o -Oend.o -o argv && echo ok!
ok!
```

## A better solution
Looking through the bcc source code I found the the following dummy declaration
is used to satisfy the linker requirement in instances like this:

```
...
loop_safe:
  .long 0
  .text

export ___mkargv
___mkargv:      ! BCC Tells linker to init argv ... none needed.

export ___cstartup
___cstartup:        ! Crt0 startup (Linux style)
  mov   eax,[esp]
...
```

so I added the following to the MINIX crtso.s to ensure that the linker will
succeed for all MINIX programs that use argc/argc:

```
...
; Note: BCC Tells the linker to init argv by adding a global reference to its
; ___mkargv label in main. This label will never be used when using MINIX
; crtso.o, but we need to define the label to keep the linker happy.
.text
___mkargv:  ; ___mkargv will never be called
begtext:
crtso:		mov	bx,sp
		mov	cx,(bx)
...
```
